-- allium: 3

-- Module: lifecycle.
-- Scope: knarr's own life around the annotations it leaves behind. The
-- cleanup that removes an owned annotation when the pod or the annotation
-- leaves knarr's management, the marker it leaves, its retry and its order;
-- what a value a restarted knarr finds on a marked pod means and what the
-- write times ownership's Recorded marker holds mean; a restart, a crash's
-- leftovers and a down knarr; the restart threshold handed to §9.16; and
-- the uninstall procedure with its manual recovery (§3 restart criterion,
-- §5 stale state, §8, §9.12). Cleanup acts only on annotations standing
-- owned under ownership's Adoption; a yielded, conflict or foreign value
-- stays. There is no expiry by age: the reading window reconcile keeps in
-- memory is the only expiry, within a run and across a restart alike, and
-- a safe-to-evict hold cap, if ticket 25 sets one, is not an expiry
-- (Restart NoExpiry). The
-- single replica with no leader election and the gap during a Recreate
-- upgrade (§8, §9.19) are decided in the overview and stated here by
-- ticket 32 with tend on 2026-10-14.
-- Owner: ticket 23 settled every §9.12 question here with the maintainer on
-- 2026-10-13. Ticket 32 builds it. The patch a cleanup sends is ownership's
-- cleanup (ticket 22, amended by 23); whether it may go now is budget's
-- (ticket 24); the pod LIST it reads and the owner names are k8s_client's
-- (ticket 41); the target set and what a Deployment enables are config's
-- (ticket 19); the metric, the Event and the log line are observability's
-- (ticket 30); the uninstall page is ticket 34's.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One owned annotation the pod's marker holds a record for, by the short
-- name ownership's Owned carries, with where it stands under ownership's
-- Adoption. An annotation with a record stands owned, conflict or yielded;
-- unowned and foreign are the standings of an annotation with no record,
-- and such an annotation is never held.
value Held {
    name: String
    standing: owned | conflict | yielded
}

-- One pod as the last discovery cycle in which every LIST succeeded shows
-- it, built by the caller from the unselected pod LIST, the ReplicaSet
-- LIST and config's target set. deployment is the name the pod's
-- ReplicaSet's controller owner reference carries (k8s_client Owner.name),
-- the empty string when the chain names no Deployment, which is also the
-- deployment label such a pod's patches carry (Label). target is whether
-- the pod is in
-- config's target set. enabled holds the short names of the owned
-- annotations the pod's Deployment enables, pod-deletion-cost always and
-- safe-to-evict when config's Settings.safe_to_evict is true; it is empty
-- for a pod that is not a target. terminating is whether the pod's
-- deletionTimestamp is set. held is one entry per owned annotation the
-- marker holds a record for, none when the marker is absent or ownership's
-- read finds it unusable or edited; it follows the newest API-returned
-- view of the pod under ownership's Adoption, the cycle's LIST or knarr's
-- own later accepted patch response, while target, enabled and terminating
-- are the cycle's.
value View {
    pod_name: String
    deployment: String
    target: Boolean
    enabled: List<String>
    terminating: Boolean
    held: List<Held>
}

-- One removal a cleanup makes: the owned annotation, by short name, to
-- remove from the pod together with its record, and the deployment of the
-- View that made it, which the Removal keeps while it is pending or in
-- flight, so Done and Label still find its Deployment once the pod has
-- left the pod LIST.
value Removal {
    pod_name: String
    name: String
    deployment: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract Cleanup {
    due: (view: View) -> List<Removal>
    done: (views: List<View>, outstanding: List<Removal>, deployment: String) -> Boolean

    @invariant Scope
        -- A cleanup is made when a pod or an annotation leaves knarr's
        -- management, and at no other time: knarr's own shutdown, graceful
        -- or not, removes nothing, so a Recreate upgrade causes no removal
        -- and no rewrite burst. due is derived from the View alone, with
        -- no memory of earlier cycles: for a view that is not terminating,
        -- it returns one Removal per held annotation standing owned whose
        -- name is not in enabled, which is empty for a pod that is not a
        -- target (its Deployment opted out, was deleted, or never was one)
        -- and lacks safe-to-evict for a target whose Deployment removed
        -- that opt-in; for a terminating view it returns nothing, since no
        -- patch is sent to a terminating pod (k8s_client
        -- NoPatchWhileTerminating) and no new one is awaited; a Removal
        -- of its pod already pending or in flight is Done's to account
        -- for. A
        -- held annotation standing conflict or yielded is left as found:
        -- no yield, no Event, the record stays, and a later re-opt-in sees
        -- the conflict and yields then under ownership's Yield. A pod whose
        -- marker is absent, unusable or edited holds nothing, so nothing is
        -- due for it whatever it carries. A record whose value is null and
        -- whose annotation is absent stands owned (ownership Adoption), so
        -- it is due too: the cleanup is then the marker-only patch
        -- ownership's Released describes. This covers the opt-out made
        -- while knarr was down, which no event could name, and the record
        -- reconcile's Neutral leaves when it removes a disabled annotation
        -- before cleanup reaches it. Every view is derived afresh at each
        -- discovery cycle in which every LIST succeeded, and its target,
        -- enabled and terminating stand until the next such cycle replaces
        -- it, so a failed cycle changes nothing (config Discovery, budget
        -- ListCadence); its held, and the marker and annotations the
        -- cleanup patch is built from, follow the newest API-returned view
        -- of the pod (ownership Adoption), so a patch of knarr's own that
        -- lands between cycles does not cost the cleanup a 422.
    @invariant Marker
        -- A cleanup removes each due annotation and drops its record from
        -- the marker in one patch, ownership's cleanup with the due names
        -- as owned; when no record remains the marker annotation is removed
        -- too, so a pod every annotation of which knarr released carries
        -- nothing of knarr's and is adoptable again. A yielded or conflict
        -- record keeps the marker, so a yield survives an opt-out and a
        -- re-opt-in, as ownership's Yield promises for the pod's life. The
        -- record is dropped rather than nulled because a null applied
        -- record with an absent annotation stands owned under ownership's
        -- Adoption: a nulled record would keep a disabled annotation owned,
        -- asked about and counted for the pod's life. A re-opt-in while a
        -- cleanup is in flight costs one extra write and nothing else: the
        -- accepted cleanup's response is the newest view (ownership
        -- Adoption), the next cycle finds a target with no marker, unowned
        -- and adoptable, and it is rewritten at its first valid reading at
        -- or above the first threshold (cost_mapping Crossing).
    @invariant Retry
        -- A cleanup that is not accepted, a 422, a rejection, a 429 or a
        -- transport failure alike, leaves nothing behind for scheduling but
        -- the pod's last attempt time: knarr remembers no failure to decide
        -- when or whether to retry. The rejection run (observability
        -- Cadence) and the failed operations until the next LIST (ownership
        -- Rejected) are kept as those clauses say. The Removal stays
        -- due while the view that made it stands and is due again from
        -- every later view that names it (budget Pending keeps the entry
        -- while due names the pod), so it is retried while the pod lives,
        -- is not terminating and is still out of scope, and it goes no
        -- sooner than the patch interval, counted from the pod's last
        -- patch attempt whatever its outcome or purpose (budget PerPod),
        -- and never inside a pause (budget BackOff). For a target the
        -- interval is its Deployment's override or else
        -- config.patch_interval; for a pod that is not a target it is
        -- config.patch_interval, since its Deployment is not in the
        -- selected LIST and its override cannot be read. A rejected cleanup
        -- raises CleanupRejected once per
        -- rejection run, the run observability's Events Rejected defines,
        -- which only an accepted patch to the pod, the pod terminating or
        -- the pod leaving the pod LIST ends, and counts on
        -- knarr_patch_rejections_total with purpose cleanup (observability
        -- Events Rejected and CleanupRejected, Catalogue Patches); a
        -- permanent rejection, by an admission webhook, keeps the
        -- Deployment's series alive at one attempt per pod per interval
        -- until the operator removes by hand what the Event's message
        -- names, the annotation left or, for a marker-only cleanup that
        -- would have removed the marker, the marker, or the pod goes; a
        -- marker-only cleanup that would have rewritten the marker names
        -- nothing to remove (observability CleanupRejected) and is retried
        -- while the pod lives.
    @invariant Order
        -- A due cleanup is a pending change under budget's Pending, with
        -- purpose cleanup, served oldest-due first among every pending
        -- change by budget's order and bounded by the bucket and the pause
        -- like any other patch; nothing gives a cleanup priority or holds
        -- it back. A pod due both an apply and a cleanup, as when the
        -- safe-to-evict opt-in is removed while the cost crosses a
        -- threshold, makes two patches, each with its own purpose, the
        -- second waiting the patch interval. After a restart the first
        -- discovery cycle in which every LIST succeeded derives every
        -- cleanup due at once, all with the same due_since, and the bucket
        -- alone bounds them (budget Restart): at the defaults an install
        -- with 500 pods to clean takes about 100 seconds.
    @invariant Done
        -- done is true for a Deployment when no live, non-terminating view
        -- whose deployment is that name has a non-empty due, and
        -- outstanding, the cleanups pending or in flight, holds no Removal
        -- whose deployment is that name, so a cleanup in flight for a pod
        -- that has since left the pod LIST holds the Deployment until it is
        -- answered. done reads only each Removal's deployment: the caller
        -- builds outstanding from budget's Pending, which stores no
        -- annotation name, and from the patches in flight, each with the
        -- deployment of the View that made it. It is what observability's
        -- SeriesLife waits for before retiring the Deployment's series once
        -- it is not in the target set, so an opt-out's cleanups are counted
        -- before the series go. A terminating view makes nothing due and
        -- so holds nothing up by itself; a Removal of its pod that is
        -- still outstanding is awaited like any other, pending until
        -- budget's Pending drops it, since due no longer names the pod, or
        -- in flight until it is answered.
    @invariant Label
        -- A cleanup patch counts on knarr_patches_total and
        -- knarr_patch_rejections_total with purpose cleanup and the
        -- deployment label its Removal carries, copied from the View that
        -- made it: the Deployment's name for a
        -- target, and for a pod that is not one the name its ReplicaSet's
        -- controller owner reference carries (k8s_client Owner.name), since
        -- the opted-out Deployment is not in the selected LIST and config's
        -- ReplicaSet reads no name. A marked pod whose chain names no
        -- Deployment, after a cascade-orphan deletion, is still cleaned up,
        -- counted with deployment empty; those series live and retire as a
        -- Deployment's do, under the empty name. Each cleanup patch logs at
        -- debug as every patch does (observability Logs Levels). No signal
        -- is added: cleanup is visible on knarr_patches_total,
        -- knarr_patch_rejections_total, knarr_annotations and
        -- CleanupRejected.
    @invariant Pure
        -- due and done are functions of their arguments alone: the views
        -- arrive built, and neither reads the cluster, a clock, config or
        -- state, or sends. Building the views from the three LISTs and the
        -- newest API-returned view of each pod, with ownership's read and
        -- standing for held, keeping the pending set
        -- and sending the patch are the discovery and patcher processes'
        -- (tickets 31 and 46), under supervision; the cleanup's operations
        -- are ownership's cleanup. Ticket 47 built the cleanup on those
        -- processes on 2026-10-15: each complete discovery cycle builds a
        -- View for every pod the unselected pod LIST shows and hands every
        -- non-empty due to the patcher with the listed pods, a cleanup turn
        -- re-derives due from the newest view and sends one patch, and
        -- done decides when a name out of the target set retires its
        -- series (reconcile Cycle).
}

contract Restart {
    @invariant Replica
        -- knarr runs as a single replica with no leader election, deployed
        -- with strategy Recreate (§8, §9.19). A Recreate upgrade leaves a
        -- short gap in which no update is made and nothing else happens:
        -- no removal, since knarr's own shutdown removes nothing (Cleanup
        -- Scope), and nothing moves (Leftovers). The gap raises no Event
        -- and no metric of its own; the new pod answers /readyz 503 until
        -- its first discovery cycle in which every LIST of it succeeds
        -- (observability Probes Readiness) and starts its counters at 0
        -- (observability Catalogue SeriesLife). Ticket 32 stated this
        -- clause with tend on 2026-10-14 from the overview's decided rows.
        -- The manifest test in scripts/checks/tests/test_deployment.py
        -- proves one replica, strategy Recreate and no leader-election
        -- resource, and the readiness latch and supervisor restart tests
        -- in test/reconciler_test.gleam prove what the new process
        -- answers.
    @invariant NoExpiry
        -- Expiry is removing a value because it is stale: for lack of a
        -- valid reading, or by the age of its write. No owned annotation
        -- expires by age, pod-deletion-cost and
        -- safe-to-evict alike, within a run or across a downtime. The
        -- reading window is the expiry: a value a restarted knarr finds on
        -- a marked pod stands, as it did before the restart, until the
        -- window elapses again in the new process (reconcile Window,
        -- Neutral and Restart) or the first valid reading corrects it
        -- (cost_mapping Crossing), whichever comes first. So nothing flaps
        -- for lack of a reading (§3), and the staleness of a value after a
        -- downtime is bounded the same way it is within a run: by the
        -- window after the first readings of the new process. A dead knarr
        -- cannot act on anything, so an expiry judged at startup from the
        -- marker's times would be a guaranteed flap after every long
        -- downtime and is rejected. A safe-to-evict hold cap, if ticket 25
        -- sets one, is not an expiry: it is safe_to_evict's policy, decided
        -- from valid readings and from time counted in memory from when the
        -- mark began, reset by a restart like the reading window, and never
        -- read from the marker's at (Informational); ticket 25 states it and
        -- what a restart does to it.
    @invariant Informational
        -- The marker's at and each record's at are the time of the write
        -- that made them (ownership Recorded), and no decision in knarr is
        -- made from their values: ownership's read parses them only to
        -- decide that the marker is usable and render round-trips them,
        -- and nothing compares them with a clock or with each other, not
        -- for expiry, scheduling (budget Restart), budgeting (budget
        -- Restart), standing (ownership Adoption) or the reading state
        -- (reconcile Restart). They tell an operator when knarr last
        -- changed the pod and each annotation. Whether a steady cost or
        -- hours of failed polls kept a value is not decided from them,
        -- and does not need to be: knarr's own health is
        -- knarr_last_discovery_timestamp_seconds and /readyz (observability
        -- Install and Probes), and a pod's reading state is
        -- knarr_pods_unknown and knarr_pods_contract_absent.
    @invariant NoStartupStep
        -- A restarted knarr has no startup pass of its own over marked
        -- pods. It reconciles them through the normal loop: the first
        -- discovery cycle in which every LIST succeeded derives the
        -- cleanups due (Cleanup Scope), every target with a pod IP joins
        -- the schedule with a fresh phase and the initial state (budget
        -- Phase, reconcile NoPodIp and Restart), polls correct values at
        -- their first valid reading,
        -- windows neutralise what stops reading valid, and the first cycle
        -- rebuilds the gauges (observability SeriesLife). Which pods are
        -- reconciled is every pod the LISTs show; the order is budget's
        -- oldest-due, bounded by the bucket.
    @invariant Leftovers
        -- An annotation left on a pod by a crash, or standing while knarr
        -- is down, is a value the new process finds and handles as NoExpiry
        -- and NoStartupStep say; nothing distinguishes a crash from a
        -- graceful stop. While knarr is down or crashlooping nothing moves:
        -- annotations go stale, and a safe-to-evict "false" can pin a node
        -- until knarr returns or an operator removes it with the per-pod
        -- command Uninstall's Recovery gives (§8 table). The pods of a
        -- Deployment that opted out during the downtime are found out of
        -- scope by the first cycle and cleaned up like any other. Neither
        -- a down knarr nor a crashlooping one raises an Event: a down
        -- knarr exports nothing; a crashlooping one answers /readyz 503
        -- until its first discovery cycle in which every LIST of it
        -- succeeds and starts its counters at 0 on each start
        -- (observability Catalogue SeriesLife); and a running knarr whose
        -- loop stalls shows as a stale
        -- knarr_last_discovery_timestamp_seconds, which is the alert, and
        -- liveness never restarts on it (observability Probes Liveness).
        -- Ticket 32 added the signals with tend on 2026-10-14.
    @invariant Threshold
        -- The restart threshold §9.16 sets for the §3 criterion, measured
        -- by the harness ticket 33 builds against the fake worker's
        -- control endpoint, has three parts, after knarr is stopped and a
        -- new process answers /readyz 200. (a) For a Deployment whose
        -- opt-ins did not change and every worker of which holds a cost
        -- whose mapped value (cost_mapping map) equals its applied value
        -- through the downtime and the run, the new process's
        -- knarr_patches_total with that deployment label stays 0 for 300
        -- seconds after readiness. (b) A Deployment opted out during the
        -- downtime has every owned annotation standing owned removed from
        -- its live pods, and the marker of each pod where no record
        -- remains, within two list intervals of readiness, 120 seconds at
        -- the defaults. (c) A worker whose cost changed during the downtime
        -- so that its mapped value differs from its applied value
        -- (cost_mapping Crossing) is rewritten within one poll interval
        -- plus its jitter plus one patch interval of readiness, about 100
        -- seconds at the defaults. One restart run asserts all three, (a)
        -- on the Deployments the run leaves untouched and (b) and (c) on
        -- others. The windows of (b) and (c) are bounds with slack for a
        -- harness run in which no patch is answered 429, so budget's
        -- BackOff never pauses, and the patches due after readiness fit
        -- the bucket within the window: after a restart every pod is
        -- patchable at once (budget Restart) and the first poll is due
        -- within one interval (budget Phase), so the derivable times are
        -- shorter, and the slack absorbs the bucket. A pause, or more
        -- patches due than the bucket serves in the window (Cleanup
        -- Order), lengthens them, and the threshold does not cover it.
}

contract Uninstall {
    @invariant Procedure
        -- Uninstall is: opt every Deployment out, wait for cleanup, inspect
        -- and release remaining markers under Recovery, then delete knarr.
        -- The operator removes knarr.io/enabled from every opted-in
        -- Deployment (config OptIn) and waits for a fresh successful cycle
        -- after the opt-out: knarr_last_discovery_timestamp_seconds is
        -- later than the label removal and knarr_targets with kind
        -- deployments is 0 (observability Install). Then no
        -- knarr_annotations series with standing owned may be above 0 for
        -- the install. Series read 0 or are absent once a Deployment's
        -- cleanup is done (observability SeriesLife); absence before the
        -- first discovery after a restart is not proof of cleanup.
        -- A kubectl get pods -o jsonpath over knarr.io/applied lists every
        -- remaining marker, including a yield, conflict, marker-only
        -- pending cleanup, or unusable or edited marker. The operator
        -- inspects each as Recovery says; a marker alone is not evidence
        -- of ownership of either annotation. Only after cleanup and
        -- inspection does the operator delete the install with kubectl
        -- delete -k. Deleting knarr before the wait leaves owned
        -- annotations standing under Restart Leftovers. A value without a
        -- usable marker is foreign (ownership Adoption) and is left alone
        -- unless independent evidence confirms it is a knarr leftover.
        -- The page is ticket 34's; its adversarial review corrected the
        -- blanket release command on 2026-10-15 without changing automatic
        -- cleanup or any controller behaviour.
    @invariant Recovery
        -- Manual recovery after a dead install or a rejected cleanup uses
        -- the same inspection. For a usable canonical marker (ownership
        -- Recorded), remove only annotations whose valid applied record
        -- equals the current value (ownership Adoption and Applied),
        -- together with the marker once every record has been accounted
        -- for. A null applied record with an absent annotation needs only
        -- its record released. Preserve yielded, conflict and foreign
        -- values: an applied record that differs from the current value
        -- does not prove ownership. A marker with no safe-to-evict record
        -- never authorizes removing that key; ticket 25's policy is
        -- deferred. For an unusable or edited marker, confirm leftovers
        -- from independent evidence before removing values, otherwise
        -- preserve them. A CleanupRejected message can identify knarr's
        -- keys to remove and another writer's values to preserve; a
        -- marker-only rewrite rejection is not permission to discard
        -- records still needed for another cleanup. Use the inspected
        -- pod's resourceVersion as a precondition on manual annotation
        -- changes, and re-inspect if it changes. Once no owned value
        -- remains, remove any residual marker alone while preserving the
        -- other writers' annotations; present values then stand foreign
        -- to a running knarr. To deliberately reset another writer's
        -- protection requires a separate operator decision, not uninstall.
}
